Answer: Encapsulation is the process of binding variables and methods together as a single unit and providing controlled access to important data. Encapsulation is achieved by: Declaring important data members as private. Providing public setter methods to assign values. Providing public getter methods to retrieve values. Using only private does not complete encapsulation because it only restricts direct access. Similarly, using only getters and setters without making the variables private does not provide proper encapsulation. The main advantage of encapsulation is data security. Example:

```java
class Student {
private int id;
private String name;
public void setData(int a, String b) {
id = a;
name = b;
}
public int getId() {
return id;
}
public String getName() {
return name;
}
}
```

The object can be created and accessed through the public methods:

```java
class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Student s = new Student();
s.setData(10, "Ramesh");
System.out.println(s.getId() + " " + s.getName());
}
}
```

Output: 10 Ramesh

Answer: A constructor is a specialized method-like member that: Has the same name as the class. Does not have a return type. Is executed at the time of object creation. Is used for object creation and initialization of instance variables. For example:

```java
class Student {
int id;
String name;
```

Student(int a, String b) {

```java
id = a;
name = b;
}
}
```

The constructor is: Student(int a, String b) because its name is the same as the class name and it has no return type. A constructor is executed when an object is created:

```java
Student s = new Student(10, "Ramesh");
```

This allows the instance variables to be initialized during object creation instead of requiring a separate setter call.

Answer: Consider:

```java
class Student {
int id;
String name;
```

Student(int a, String b) {

```java
id = a;
name = b;
}
}
```

The object can be created as:

```java
Student s = new Student(10, "Ramesh");
```

The object is first created in heap memory. The constructor is then called with 10 and "Ramesh". Inside the constructor: a → 10 b → Ramesh The values are assigned:

```java
id = a;
name = b;
```

Therefore, the object is completely initialized before the reference is assigned to s. This is different from creating the object first and then calling a setter:

```java
Student s = new Student();
s.setData(10, "Ramesh");
```

The constructor allows the required values to be supplied directly during object creation.

Answer: A normal setter is a method used to assign values after an object has been created. Example:

```java
Student s = new Student();
s.setData(10, "Ramesh");
```

A constructor is executed during object creation itself. Example:

```java
Student s = new Student(10, "Ramesh");
```

The important difference is the time of execution: Constructor Setter Executed during object creation Called after object creation Same name as the class Can have any valid method name Has no return type Can have a return type Used for object creation and initialization Used to modify/set data The constructor can therefore be viewed as a specialized setter because it initializes instance data at the time of object creation.

Answer: If a class does not contain any constructor, the compiler adds a zero-parameterized constructor. This compiler-added zero-parameterized constructor is called the default constructor. For example:

```java
class Fan {
int numberOfWings;
String brand;
}
```

The compiler conceptually provides a constructor similar to: Fan() {

```java
super();
}
```

Therefore, this object creation is possible:

```java
Fan fan = new Fan();
```

The instance variables receive their default values: numberOfWings → 0 brand → null

Answer: The compiler adds a default constructor only when the class does not contain any constructor. For example:

```java
class Fan {
int numberOfWings;
String brand;
}
```

A default constructor is added. However, if a constructor is explicitly written:

```java
class Fan {
int numberOfWings;
String brand;
```

Fan(int a, String b) {

```java
numberOfWings = a;
brand = b;
}
}
```

the compiler does not add a zero-parameterized default constructor. Therefore:

```java
Fan fan = new Fan();
```

will produce a constructor-related compilation error. The available constructor must be called with the required parameters:

```java
Fan fan = new Fan(10, "Ramesh");
```

