Answer: super() is used to call the constructor of the parent class. For example:

```java
class Fan {
```

Fan() {

```java
super();
}
}
```

If Fan does not explicitly extend another class, its parent is Object. Therefore, super() in the Fan constructor calls the constructor of the Object class.

Answer: Constructor chaining is the process of calling one constructor from another constructor. Two forms were identified: Calling a parent-class constructor from a child-class constructor using super(). Calling another constructor in the same class using this(). Example of parent constructor chaining:

```java
class Parent {
```

Parent() {

```java
System.out.println("Parent");
}
}
class Child extends Parent {
```

Child() {

```java
super();
System.out.println("Child");
}
}
```

When a Child object is created, the parent constructor is executed through super().

Answer: Local constructor chaining means calling another constructor present in the same class using this(). Example:

```java
class Student {
```

Student() {

```java
this(10);
}
```

Student(int id) {

```java
System.out.println(id);
}
}
```

Here, the no-argument constructor calls the parameterized constructor of the same class using:

```java
this(10);
```

This is different from super(), which is used to call a parent-class constructor.

Answer: Shadowing occurs when a local variable or parameter has the same name as an instance variable. Example:

```java
class Student {
int id;
```

Student(int id) {

```java
id = id;
}
}
```

Here, both id references inside the constructor refer to the parameter. Therefore, the instance variable does not receive the intended value. The this keyword is used to refer to the current object's instance variable:

```java
class Student {
int id;
```

Student(int id) {

```java
this.id = id;
}
}
```

Here: this.id → instance variable id → local parameter The this keyword represents the current object.

Answer: A static block contains statements that should execute before the main() method executes. Example:

```java
class Demo {
static {
System.out.println("Static block");
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
System.out.println("Main method");
}
}
```

The static block is also called a static initializer. It can be used to initialize static variables.

Answer: Object is the default parent class of a Java class when no other parent class is explicitly specified. For example:

```java
class Fan {
}
```

The compiler conceptually treats this as:

```java
class Fan extends Object {
}
```

Therefore, Fan inherits from Object. The Object class is an inbuilt Java class and contains its own constructor and other members.

Answer: If no parent class is specified after the class name, the class implicitly extends Object. For example:

```java
class Fan {
int numberOfWings;
String brand;
}
```

is conceptually treated as:

```java
class Fan extends Object {
int numberOfWings;
String brand;
}
```

Therefore, Object becomes the parent of Fan.

Answer: Constructor chaining ensures that the initialization performed by parent classes is completed before the child class object is fully initialized. For example, suppose a parent class contains:

```java
class Demo {
int a;
int b;
```

Demo() {

```java
a = 10;
b = 20;
}
}
```

If a child class inherits these properties, the parent constructor initializes them. This supports code reusability because the child class does not need to duplicate the parent's initialization logic. Without constructor chaining, the purpose of inheriting and reusing the parent's initialization would be weakened.

